# Architecture Principles

`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` is the request-path description; this rule is what a
change must keep true.

## 1. Read-only, by construction

- There is exactly one tool class. `internal/tools.ReadOnlyTools` lists every
  tool; `readOnlyTool()` gives each the annotations `readOnlyHint: true`,
  `destructiveHint: false`, `idempotentHint: true`, `openWorldHint: false` and
  a strict input schema. `TestEveryToolIsReadOnlyAndStrict` compares the list
  with the registered tools. A tool that writes, or hands out credentials,
  does not belong in this server.
- Read-only is enforced in layers (role, session defaults, `BEGIN READ ONLY`
  + always `ROLLBACK`, single-statement extended protocol, `sqlguard`
  classifier, cursor-bounded rows). Never weaken one layer because another
  one "already" covers it; the classifier is the helpful error, layers 1-4
  are the guarantee.
- No `--read-only` / `--non-destructive` switch (unlike mcp-capi and
  mcp-kubernetes): there is nothing to switch. The server logs
  `Write policy readOnly=true writeMode=none` at startup.

## 2. Identity

- Every tool call resolves the caller first and fails closed without one;
  `LocalCaller` ("local") exists only for stdio and `OAUTH_ENABLED=false`.
- `allowedGroups` / `allowedUsers` are checked on every call, and
  `timescale_list_databases` hides what the caller may not use.
- The database sees the person: `application_name = mcp-timescale/<email>`
  for the transaction, one structured audit line per call.

## 3. Where things live

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `cmd/` | cobra: `serve` (default), `version`, `self-update` (cosign-verified) |
| `pkg/project/` | build identifiers stamped at link time; the only version source |
| `internal/config/` | `databases.yaml` loading and validation |
| `internal/sqlguard/` | SQL classifier |
| `internal/timescale/` | pools, attributed read-only sessions, catalog, value encoding |
| `internal/tools/` | the MCP tools, argument validation, audit |
| `internal/server/` | OAuth (mcp-oauth), transports, caller context, metrics |
| `helm/mcp-timescale/` | chart; `tests/` are helm-unittest suites, `ci/ci-values.yaml` renders everything |

## 4. Testing standards

- Unit tests are deterministic and need no database (unreachable loopback
  ports fail fast); `make test-integration` runs the real suite against a
  throwaway TimescaleDB container.
- New tool: add it to `ReadOnlyTools` and the README table, cover the
  argument validation and the allowlist path, and extend the integration
  suite if it touches SQL.
- Chart change: `make helm-lint`, `make helm-template`, `make helm-test`;
  `values.schema.json` is regenerated by the pre-commit hook, never by hand.

## 5. Results for a model

Tools return one JSON document as text content (no `structuredContent`);
catalog results are objects keyed by column name, `timescale_query` returns
`columns` + positional `rows`. Values are encoded for a model (RFC 3339
timestamps, `numeric` as strings, `bytea` as base64).
